tt-local-generator (0.98.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * feat(create): watch an AnimateDiff video form as it generates. The result
    panel showed a spinner and a status line; it now shows the animation itself,
    updating each denoising step, with "Denoising step N/M" beneath it. Driven
    by tt-animatediff's runner, which emits a rolling latent-preview GIF and a
    "PREVIEW: <step>/<total> <path>" line per step over the stdout channel the
    panel already drains for per-chip progress.
    The preview is CPU-side in the runner (3 of 4 latent channels mapped to RGB,
    no VAE decode, no device work), so it costs the hardware pipeline nothing —
    measured ~3% wall-clock on CPU and no measurable cost on Blackhole.
    Multi-chip runs preview chip 0 only: each chip denoises its own segment, so
    letting all four write the single rolling image would flicker between
    unrelated latents.
    Verified end to end on real Blackhole hardware (9/9 preview lines, 11.0s,
    2.8s/frame) and on CPU.

