 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 04 Aug 2026 20:15:00 +0000

tt-local-generator (0.66.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * fix(tt-tv): persistent kiosk window — the real fix for "reopened TT-TV won't
    close". Live process inspection (py-spy/proc) showed the main loop was
    HEALTHY and the bug was independent of how many videos played: destroying
    and recreating the kiosk window stranded GStreamer's gtkglsink GL context in
    its process-wide GstGLDisplay cache, so the SECOND window's video couldn't
    embed and became a bare, app-id-less GL surface ("W" fallback icon) with no
    close handler — only quitting the app tore it down. TT-TV now uses ONE
    persistent window: closing it HIDES it and soft-stops playback/generation
    (new AttractorWindow.stop()/_teardown_playback()), and reopening SHOWS the
    same window and restarts (start() is now restartable). One window = one GL
    context for the app's lifetime, so a reopen never recreates GL and can't
    break; hide always closes cleanly. Bookkeeping (queue purge, Watch context)
    moves to a MainWindow on_hide callback that keeps the window reference; the
    window is only truly destroyed at app shutdown. Also fixes screensaver
    D-Bus unsubscribe to track (connection, sub_id) pairs so restart doesn't
    accumulate duplicate handlers.
  * Reverts the v0.65.2-4 close-mechanics theories (destroy-dispatch, slot-leak,
    app_id timing) as primary causes — they were adjacent; the slot-release
    teardown is retained inside _teardown_playback.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 04 Aug 2026 19:30:00 +0000

tt-local-generator (0.65.4) noble; urgency=medium

  * fix(tt-tv): associate the kiosk window with the Gtk.Application at
    CONSTRUCTION (new `application=` arg to AttractorWindow), before `_build()`/
    `maximize()` can realize it — instead of calling set_application() after
    construction. On Wayland the xdg-toplevel app_id (KDE taskbar icon) is fixed
    at first realize; a warm/second launch could realize during construction,
    BEFORE the post-hoc set_application landed, so the second TT-TV window got
    the generic GTK fallback icon AND wasn't fully owned by the app — which is
    the window that then refused to close. Setting the application pre-realize
    makes every launch identical. (Follow-up to the v0.65.3 pipeline-leak fix;
    the user pinpointed the reopened window by its wrong icon.)

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 04 Aug 2026 18:40:00 +0000

tt-local-generator (0.65.3) noble; urgency=medium

  * fix(tt-tv): actually fix the "reopened TT-TV window won't close" bug. Root
    cause (found via a deeper trace): AttractorWindow._on_destroy released the
    two video-slot GStreamer pipelines ONLY on macOS (`if _USE_SYSTEM_PLAYER`),
    so on Linux every close leaked two live Gtk.Video pipelines. Accumulating
    leaked pipelines flood the GLib main loop with gst_poll assertion failures
    and exhaust fds, so a reopened kiosk's close/destroy work never gets
    serviced — X / Stop / Escape all stop responding and only quitting the app
    tears it down. _on_destroy now releases BOTH slots (and the graveyard
    widgets) on every platform via _unload_slot_video, mirroring the screen-lock
    release path that already did this correctly.
  * Reverted the earlier close-request GLib.idle_add deferral (v0.65.2): the
    destroy-during-dispatch theory was wrong, and deferring made closing depend
    on the very idle queue the leak was starving. Destroy is synchronous again.
  * Hardening: _on_attractor_closed now identity-guards the window reference so
    a late destroy from a previous instance can't wipe a freshly-reopened one.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 04 Aug 2026 18:05:00 +0000

tt-local-generator (0.65.2) noble; urgency=medium

  * fix(tt-tv): after starting TT-TV, closing it, and reopening, the window
    could stop responding to close (X / Stop / Escape) until the whole app was
    shut down. The close-request handler destroyed the window synchronously
    inside the close-request signal dispatch; that destroy-during-dispatch (the
    same hazard the Escape handler already defers for) corrupted GTK's close/
    event routing for the NEXT attractor window. The handler now defers the
    destroy via GLib.idle_add so the close-request emission fully unwinds first.

